🐛 Bug
Empirically, the following script gives wrong information on Tensor. This is broken for both profiling executor as well as legacy executor (but very likely due to different reasons):


Profiling executor seems to have lost device information for tensors in the graph, hence blocking the fusion because it requires restrict device check.


Moreover, if we switch the flag and set flag = False to use legacy executor, the fusion still failes because tensors are wrongfully marked as cpu tensor instead of cuda tensor. (so if we lift set torch._C._jit_override_can_fuse_on_cpu(True), the fusion will succeed.)


import torch

flag = True
torch._C._jit_set_profiling_mode(flag)
torch._C._jit_set_profiling_executor(flag)
torch._C._jit_override_can_fuse_on_cpu(False)

def dropout_training(x):
    return torch.nn.functional.dropout(x)

x = torch.randn(100, 100, requires_grad=True, device="cuda")
scripted = torch.jit.script(dropout_training)
o = scripted(x)
o = scripted(x)
print("training graph: ", scripted.graph_for(x))

